.TH LIBGXWI 3 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi 0.0.7"
.SH NAME
libgxwi \- connect C applications to a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <peios/gxwi.h>
.PP
.B cc application.c -lgxwi
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libgxwi
provides the C application binary interface for desktop connections, surface requests, shell requests, and received events.
Its shared-library SONAME is
.BR libgxwi.so.1 .
The header defines ABI version 1 as
.BR PEIOS_GXWI_ABI_VERSION .
HTTP serving, HTML rendering, and settings helpers belong to the Rust
.B gxwi
framework, not this library.
.SS Connection and serving
.B peios_gxwi_connect
connects to GXWI_DISPLAY and returns an owned opaque display handle.
It sets Identification before connecting.
.B peios_gxwi_display_destroy
releases the handle.
.PP
.B peios_gxwi_serve
returns a close-on-exec stream descriptor owned by the caller and sends its peer to the desktop.
The caller serves HTTP/2 on that descriptor and closes it when finished.
.SS Surface requests
.B peios_gxwi_create_live
sends a live surface identifier, title, role, edge, socket path, and stylesheet paths.
.B peios_gxwi_create_raw
sends a raw window identifier, title, and page path.
.B peios_gxwi_title
changes a surface title, and
.B peios_gxwi_destroy
requests removal of the application's surface.
.PP
Roles are WINDOW, PANEL, OVERLAY, BACKGROUND, and DIALOG constants from the header.
Edges are DEFAULT, TOP, BOTTOM, LEFT, and RIGHT.
The compositor decides whether a request is valid for the connection.
.SS Shell requests
.B peios_gxwi_start
requests program startup with an argument array.
.B peios_gxwi_close
requests closing a desktop surface, whose owning application decides how to handle it.
.B peios_gxwi_log_off
requests ending the desktop session.
These functions report request transmission, not compositor authorization or completion.
.SS Events
.B peios_gxwi_receive
blocks for the next packet and returns an independently owned immutable event.
.B peios_gxwi_event_kind
identifies CLOSE, SURFACES, or OTHER events.
.B peios_gxwi_event_close
reads the close surface identifier.
.B peios_gxwi_event_surface_count
and
.B peios_gxwi_event_surface
read the surface list.
Unknown, malformed, or truncated messages produce OTHER; entries with unknown roles are omitted from lists.
Truncated descriptor control data is rejected rather than accepting a partial message.
.PP
Listed entries contain desktop identifiers, title, role, icon, application and workspace identifiers, and optional own-surface or application declarations.
Test the HAS_OWN and HAS_APPLICATION flags before reading the corresponding optional fields.
.B peios_gxwi_event_destroy
releases an event and invalidates its borrowed string views.
Destroying a display does not invalidate an event.
.SS Memory and concurrency
Text uses UTF-8 spans with explicit byte lengths, without a required terminator.
Embedded NUL bytes are supported.
A NULL span or array pointer is valid only when its length is zero.
Input storage is borrowed until the call returns.
Output parameters must be writable and must not alias inputs.
.PP
Display operations can run concurrently.
Concurrent receive calls consume separate packets in an unspecified completion order.
.B peios_gxwi_stop_listening
wakes receivers with end of file while leaving sending available.
No display operation can overlap destruction of that display.
.PP
Events can be read concurrently but cannot be read while being destroyed.
NULL destruction is a no-op.
Invalid non-NULL pointers, double destruction, and use after destruction are caller errors.
No callbacks are invoked.
.SH SECURITY
Connecting or sending shell requests does not grant shell authority.
The compositor remains responsible for deciding which connections can perform shell operations.
Validate application data and enforce backend authorization independently of displayed controls.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Ordinary operations return 0 on success and -1 with errno on failure.
Output parameters remain unchanged on failure.
.B peios_gxwi_receive
returns 1 with an event, 0 at end of file with a NULL event, or -1 on failure.
.B peios_gxwi_abi_version
returns the supported ABI version.
Destruction functions return no value.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
Invalid UTF-8, unknown role or edge, wrong event kind, or another invalid argument.
.TP
.B ERANGE
A surface-list index is out of range.
.TP
.B ENOENT
GXWI_DISPLAY is absent, or a required filesystem object cannot be found.
.TP
.B EIO
An operation failed without a more specific operating-system error.
.PP
System-call errors are preserved, including connection, allocation, and descriptor errors where applicable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
Path of the desktop socket to connect to.
The API does not adopt arbitrary caller-supplied descriptors as displays.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gxwid (8),
.BR gxwi-hello-app (1)
